  "slug": "protein-golgi-1670",
  "summary": "Quantitative proteomics paired with network-level and transcript-aware computational tools is expanding how molecular organization is read across cellular scales.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational readout of molecular organization from quantitative proteomic or transcript-level data. DIFFRACT is the only item that directly couples quantitative proteomics with protein-protein interaction network structure, applying local fractal dimension to detect functional reorganization. tWhEEL works at the transcript level, inferring gene redundancy from transcriptome assembly to score CRISPR knockout feasibility. The Hippo mechanosignaling study maps regulatory wiring during endothelial differentiation, and the chimeric RNA atlas links 569,640 candidates across 1019 cancer cell lines to cell fitness through dependency screening. Only the first item addresses protein organization within the cell, so the shared thread is partial rather than universal. What is new here is the use of local network geometry as a readout of functional state within an interaction network.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-06"
}
